{
  "_readme": "Every entry: query text, and an expectation. expect='answer' means it must return a non-null result (a real answer or a clarifying question -- both count as 'the bot understood something'). expect='null' means it must NOT match anything (a genuinely unanswerable or out-of-scope query -- returning an answer here would be a false positive, same bug class as the who-has-Jerome-beat incident). contains (optional) is a substring that must appear in the answer, for cases where 'it answered SOMETHING' isn't enough -- e.g. confirming direction (least vs most) or which manager. not_contains (optional) catches the opposite: a substring that must NOT appear, for confirming a wrong-direction or wrong-branch answer didn't sneak back in. Placeholders like {LATEST_FINISHED_SEASON} in contains/not_contains are resolved from raw.json at run time (see run_ask_tests.py). suggest (optional, null cases only): the miss must also come with at least one 'Did you mean' suggestion. The runner reports failures in three classes: WRONG (answered when it shouldn't have, or the answer failed contains/not_contains -- the serious kind), MISSED (no answer where one was expected), ERROR (threw).",
